ience so as a reminder you can submit  your questions using the q  a box on your left  okay so it looks like the first question  is for stephanie  um stephanie what kind of data cleanup  is required  for a sample dna degradation  yes so um for the cleanup of the data  basically you're going to have to go and  open up the raw data files  inside of gene marker um that would be  standard as far as data review from  any analyst in a standard lab and you'll  go through and resolve the flags or  uncertain allele calls within the system  as jamie talked about a lot of those  might be you know pull up artifacts that  you might need to  clean up or more commonly you will have  like heterozygous imbalances  or inconclusive homozygous calls that  just need to either be confirmed uh  or ignored for the purposes of the  comparison  that you're actually gonna do  great thank you um the next question is  for dave  dave how did the new task based training  contribute to your productivity increase  yeah the new task-based training for us  meant that we could take analysts  who um would ordinarily have had to take  several  months um if not longer to get into the  workflow and make a real contribution  and a difference  to our productivity and it let us focus  on the areas that we had real  bottlenecks in real needs  in and maybe even new technology  introduced in and we wanted to put  people straight into those areas  so that we could relief our bottlenecks  there and so for a couple of reasons  that made a huge impact  one and certainly new analysts who are  coming into the process  are useful more quickly than they would  have ordinarily been  and that's encouraging on the individual  level that also made a big difference  at the wide end productivity level too  and secondly it allowed us to implement  more of what we wanted to do more  quickly than waiting  on everyone being trained through the  entire process  interesting thank you um stephanie this  is a kind of a two-part question  did you have to perform special  protocols offline before processing the  samples  and how did the non-bone sample perform  with the rapid hit id did you have to do  any special pre-processing steps  um what did the results look like  okay so as far as the processing before  sampling so  at the autopsy level essentially what we  had them do was  target the best portion and most of the  time that was the deepest portion of the  tissue or the bone  to get the most clean sample if the  sample that was acquired  was a relatively viable tissue as well  as  you know slightly hydrated and  especially if you can see any red or  blood in in the sample  you can just go ahead and directly apply  that sample  then if it's either like in this case  where we had the burnt samples or really  dried out or anything like that  doing that pre-wash that i discussed in  the presentation so it was just  adding about uh 500 microliters to an  eppendorf tube  doing a quick wash hand canting that and  going ahead and loading it after that  that helped to both clean the sample  and rehydrate the sample so it could be  a little bit more available for the  processing  and then as similar with the the brain  tissue that we saw  if it was really dirty it was just a  matter of really targeting the cleanest  sample that we could find  um to go on to the next one the non-bone  samples performance  uh it worked really well i think the the  highest  variability that we saw was um  especially those highly degraded muscles  but for the most part again we had 28  different samples that were  really wide ranging on the degradation  level that we had  and all of them gave at least enough  allele calls to be able to  go ahead and make that comparison with  our n of seven individuals to choose  from  um and i think i already covered the  rest of that other question  okay great thank you um jamie question  for you do most labs see both pure dye  and instrument specific pull up  yeah so i think when you're talking  about pure dye that would be our  traditional spectral pull-up that you  see and then the instrument specific  as i talked about all depends on the  movement of the fluorescent molecules  across the detection window and how the  data is captured on the ccd  on the 3500 absolutely if you  view your raw data and you start looking  at pull up closer in your raw data  you're definitely going to see both  effects you'll see the  traditional pull-up peaks that center  under the parent peak  and then you'll see the sinusoidal shape  of the um  instrument specific pull-up types on the  seek studio it's a bit different the  optics are different and so you don't  see the same  instrument specific pull-up so on the  seek studio the  pull-up that you're seeing is the  traditional spectral pull up that we're  used to  great thank you um dave question for you  have you had any problems with having  multiple scientists on a single case  with prosecutors or defense wanting to  have  everyone who has touched the case  testify  great question and of course it makes  attorneys very nervous  to see that lots of hands have been all  over a case  but the reality is that sure anybody can  be called to testify  if your name is on a case packet in any  way shape or form and that's just the  system we work in and i fully appreciate  and understand that  but um really the person who's writing  their opinions and the reports of those  who are generally called to testify to  what the results  mean and and what matters at the end of  that process  and so i think what happens generally is  that  yeah the very start of initiating  process like this where you got multiple  analysts really in a  technical sense through the lab touching  a case  maybe in a batch processing system that  one or two attorneys might  call on all of the analysts in the case  to come testify  or at least be available but that soon  gets old especially when  somebody in the middle of the process  wrote on tubes or  loaded the pump plate and what does it  really mean well it might mean something  if there's something to find  but generally it it means that you just  put a plate on an instrument or  that you uh that you set up an  extraction but  the real important part for the court  purposes  is to render that opinion after you've  made your interpretation on the  downstream results and so that's what  we're  seeing in general great thank you  does anyone else want to add to that  luckily so um we have a similar system  with our outsourcing lab so not internal  but when we send samples out  and for them they've actually done some  different remote testimonies for  chain of custody purposes for the larger  workflows and then had  just uh as dave was talking about the  primary analyst that's signing the  conclusions be the one that actually  does the testimony so  you know we've seen kind of similar to  where dave has um  and you know you do might have those  people needing to be available  but there might be some you know lower  level needs that we they can do i think  ours was  done with the chain of custody hearing  beforehand  and stephanie um the question what is  the size of your agency  so we're approximately a 140 uh sworn  officers so we're not  one of the the giant big ones we're  similar to ben salem  if you guys listen to the panel  discussion um so not one of the big  giant  departments but also not super small  okay  um jamie question for you if you weren't  using marketer marker on seek studio  would you um would pull up look like  with what would pull up look like with  just auto spectrum  okay so yep marker to marker correction  is the second level of pull-up reduction  on the seek studio and it's specific  for applied biosystems uh chemistry only  so in the presentation i gave the data  you saw  for the promega and kyogen kits that is  with auto spectral only but no marker to  marker  so for the kits and samples we tested um  it was a 13 percent reduction  in pull up compared to the global filer  global filer iqc  to the kits that just had auto spectral  and when comparing to ngm detect there  was  four times reduction for the ngm detect  kit using marker to marker  compared to the other kits so a definite  improvement with the benefit of marker  to marker correction  yeah okay thank you um so dave  how do you have a goal for average  turnaround time for violent crime  processing  yeah our goal is to get it done as  quickly as possible and that  is is really important to us but we have  a mandate  for in our state for sexual assault  turnaround times like most states do now  and we want to far exceed that  when i say exceed i mean come underneath  it  so our goal for those are 120 days  um but we want to quarter beside the  turnaround time of that expectation and  include all of our case requests in that  time frame so we're  really hoping and driving towards a  month to six weeks for everything that  comes in our lab  um there was an additional question and  how difficult was it for you to upgrade  um chemistry equipment and software all  at the same time  boy how long do you have um it's uh yeah  fighting off a large part of that  um is really difficult to be honest it's  difficult on everybody involved  just the logistics of it um partnering  with  folks at thermo fisher scientific just  getting that time right as  well making sure that we have the  availability in the lab and then the  you know even just the motivation and  the brain power to be able to  gather all of that information together  and make it sensible  and worthwhile yeah it was a lot it's  probably  doing all of that together was really  pushing that boundary but  i think it was i think it was worthwhile  doing it  rather than doing it in little bits and  pieces over the course of a number of  years  getting it all done and striking all  while the iron's hot i think is  gonna prove to be fruitful in the end  great okay well i think um  we're at about time i would like to  thank all of our presenters today and  thank you to the audience for all of  your questions um all of these  presentations you've heard today will be  available for on-demand boom  until june of 2021 so please be sure to  share them with any colleagues who might  be interested in any of the topics  covered in today's sessions  as a reminder the technical ask the  expert chat room is now open  so head over there to participate in the  discussions  i'm sure you won't want to miss that and  also be sure to visit the applications  exhibits and poster halls  for a wealth of content and to connect  with thermo picture representatives  thank you for attending kids virtual and  we'll see you at hits 2021.  thank you
 

